theory.intro:
In a right-angled triangle the two acute angles add to $90^{\circ}$, so each is the other's complement. The side opposite one is adjacent to the other, so the sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement. 'Cosine' is short for 'complement's sine'. These facts use an acute angle $\theta$, where $0^{\circ}<\theta<90^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc}
\coordinate (B) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (0,5);
\coordinate (C) at (3.4,0);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,left] {$c$};
\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,below] {$a$};
\draw (C)--(A) node[midway,above right] {$b$};
\coordinate (ra1) at ($(B)!0.32cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (ra2) at ($(B)!0.32cm!(C)$);
\draw (ra1)--($(ra1)+(ra2)-(B)$)--(ra2);
\pic[draw,"$\theta$",angle radius=0.8cm,angle eccentricity=1.9] {angle=B--A--C};
\pic[draw,"$90^{\circ}-\theta$",angle radius=1.1cm,angle eccentricity=2] {angle=A--C--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
In the right-angled triangle, write $\sin\theta$, $\cos\theta$ and $\sin(90^{\circ}-\theta)$ as side ratios. Hence explain why $\sin(90^{\circ}-\theta)=\cos\theta$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,3);
\coordinate (B) at (0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0);
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,left] {$q$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,below] {$p$};
\path (A)--(C) node[midway,above right] {$r$};
\draw (0,2.5) arc[start angle=270,end angle=323.13,radius=0.5];
\path (0.35,2.15) node {$\theta$};
\draw (3.3,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=143.13,radius=0.7];
\path (2.55,0.4) node {$90^{\circ}-\theta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

